"type explorer" is a notebook page
	<header level="1">
		TypeExplorer
		<header>The scopes a project parses in, as a tree, with the details of the one picked.</>
	</>

	<text class="lede" paragraph>The "Type Explorer" tab of a runner's debug pane: everything a project's code declares, and all it can use, as a tree of scopes on the left, and the details of the one picked on the right. It's how you see what spell made of your code: its types, their properties and actions, and the javascript each became.</>
	<list class="spell-meta">
		<item icon="user">Written for: anyone changing the runners' debug pane, or reading its code</>
		<item icon="play">Live: a real <text code>&lt;spell-viewer&gt;</>, its debug pane opened on this tab</>
	</>

	<section label="1. Example">
		<icon slot="icon" name="eye"/>
		<text paragraph>Solitaire, its debug pane open on "Type Explorer": the pane under the game IS this component. In the "Scopes" tree, open Card.spell under Solitaire and click Card, to see its details beside it.</>
		<example "1. Example" description="Solitaire, its debug pane on the Type Explorer tab.">
			<html>
				<spell-viewer project="@spell/examples/Solitaire" toolbar debug="explorer" height="820px"></spell-viewer>
			</html>
		</example>
		<text class="meta" paragraph>Live, from the app's element build, which this set's pages load: <text code>/element/spell.pack.js</>. <link target="src-packages-app" link="/src/app/">The app package</> builds it with <text code>yarn build:element</>.</>
	</>

	<section label="2. Using it">
		<icon slot="icon" name="code"/>
		<text paragraph>Each runner draws one in its debug pane. VS Code's runner gives it the most: the scopes the extension sends, and a way to save docstrings and refresh:</>
		<text paragraph>VSCodeRunner.tsx · the Type Explorer tab:</>
		<markdown>
			```typescript
			/** The Type Explorer, from the scopes the extension sends. */
			function explorer() {
			  return (
			    <TypeExplorer
			      tree={tree()}
			      onOpen={(href) => post({ type: "open", href })}
			      onSaveDescription={(at, text) => post({ type: "setDescription", ...at, text })}
			      loadDetails={loadDetails}
			      onRefresh={() => post({ type: "refreshScopes" })}
			      state={settings().typeExplorer}
			      onStateChange={(typeExplorer) => save({ typeExplorer })}
			    />
			  )
			}
			```
		</>
		<list bulleted>
			<item><text code>tree</> is the tree of scopes: from the language server in VS Code, or from the project's scope pack in a <link target="src-packages-app-docs-spell-viewer-html" link="/spell-viewer"><text code>&lt;spell-viewer&gt;</></>, which needs no parser in the page.</>
			<item>A scope's details are asked for with <text code>loadDetails()</> the first time it's picked, and kept until a new tree comes.</>
			<item>The right side is a <link target="src-packages-app-docs-scopedetailspane-html" link="/scope-details-pane">ScopeDetailsPane</>, made afresh for each scope picked.</>
			<item>It lists things as they're declared, with a marker for each heading they're under, or in alphabetical order: the buttons in its header switch.</>
			<item>What's picked and open is kept by each scope's path, so it stays put as new trees come in, after each run. <text code>state</> and <text code>onStateChange</> remember it, else it's kept in the browser.</>
			<item><text code>readonly</>, as in a <text code>&lt;spell-viewer&gt;</>: docstrings can't be edited.</>
			<item>Each row of its tree is a <link link="#TreeRow">TreeRow</>: its arrow opens and closes it, the rest picks it. The <link target="src-packages-app-docs-thingexplorer-html" link="/thing-explorer">ThingExplorer</>'s tree uses it too.</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="3. Props, and where it's drawn">
		<icon slot="icon" name="list check"/>
		<html>
			<ui-docs-api family="TypeExplorer"></>
		</>
		<text class="meta" paragraph>From its code, by <text code>yarn site:data</> in <link target="src-spell-app" link="/"><text code>spell-app</></>.</>
	</>

